> API and interpretation

The callback must be finite and satisfy the continuity assumptions for a
root-existence interpretation. Same-sign endpoints, zero budgets and
representational stagnation are explicit outcomes. Initial endpoints and
distinct midpoint/correction calls are captured only when actually evaluated;
reused midpoint values do not invent callback work. Width convergence and
residual convergence must be interpreted separately.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Ridders, A new algorithm for computing a single root of a real continuous function (1979)|https://doi.org/10.1109/TCS.1979.1084580]

